    {
      "@id": "card_1deb00d5-7b14-4f4b-8fba-e886c868cdad",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Walkability Without Voice",
      "description": " A city can promise walkability while allowing conditions that endanger vulnerable pedestrians and public meetings that do not let them contest the tradeoffs. The mechanism is a mismatch between the language of inclusion and the terms under which people are actually heard.",
      "x": 19708.167113631596,
      "y": 1851.3049197781525,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_ac0b628c-fb30-42a4-b561-3303e2dadb78",
        "item_390a84f0-7ed6-4610-8df7-a6052451ba5a"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_bca2ee95-81ac-4e5a-bf28-836c299aa2ef",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Truth Lacks Viral Pull",
      "description": "People trying to judge civic information face a media environment where false or manipulative content travels faster because it is more emotionally sticky, more memorable, and easier to share. The consequence is that truth struggles to compete for attention, even when it is accurate.  ",
      "x": 18017.51036730723,
      "y": -840,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_1e9046cb-0b92-4b32-b0fc-7d6dd3b4e5f4",
        "item_6413462e-1a13-43d1-9093-b0521623b942",
        "item_ed0a24ad-74a4-4ad0-b7eb-5f65ba58b9ca",
        "item_97470058-d7b3-4b45-b90e-6ef7c0c73fc7",
        "item_640c6863-f9fd-48a1-8017-0cfb28bcbe18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_8e97daae-2ab3-4b97-b2b2-74056289092a",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Rigged Before Votes / Hidden Disenfranchisement",
      "description": "Political actors and district rules shape electoral outcomes before most voters cast a ballot, so the vote arrives after key advantages are already set. The consequence is that disenfranchisement is experienced as built into the field rather than as a one-off failure.\n\nVoters are trying to tell whether they have been structurally shut out, but gerrymandering, two-party privilege, and oversized districts make political influence feel unreliable or predetermined. The consequence is a standing uncertainty about whether participation still carries equal weight.",
      "x": 15141.834135065095,
      "y": 2660.8895131857153,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_e5a57d07-a8e2-4f5d-bacb-b4ce016a62f1",
        "item_b8db4bd1-f61b-46c7-8207-b2d76ee85595"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_62f86f72-285b-4525-95db-4361cfe64719",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Perfectionist Exit from Politics",
      "description": "Some citizens disengage because politics feels imperfect, confusing, or not worth the effort, while abstention itself can still shape election outcomes. This sits alongside broader signals of low civic knowledge and weak participation, making “not voting” both a personal stance and a structural force.  ",
      "x": 16987.36770395535,
      "y": 2749.8631757625253,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_2e974732-022b-4666-b768-47d25852127f",
        "item_af86fd26-d3a3-4853-9cd7-1f36b34ca60a",
        "item_6cdb60bb-d188-4616-b1d6-2e6082a996d5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_44347b9e-520d-446d-9b32-420f55611be9",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Performative Dialogue",
      "description": "Civic actors who are expected to disagree often stage collaboration, but do not actually sustain the listening, respect, or shared problem definition that real dialogue requires. The result is that difference is either left untouched or managed as appearance.",
      "x": 15531.877977107184,
      "y": 1133.2496836335417,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_745f184c-3fb5-4cef-985e-efdb6c16aaf4",
        "item_c1f3d5af-39f4-4b72-9ba1-96a035860675",
        "item_fa9f6ff7-05e2-429b-90c8-998faa2a1a56",
        "item_4839f55d-bcfb-4db3-b714-f4bd4b130a61"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_06d62388-65e3-4bdd-b41e-e0ab3af481ef",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Trust Fractures in Public Health",
      "description": "Communities are making choices about science and health under conditions of weakened trust, where underfunded science and vaccine misinformation distort what feels credible. The consequence is not just bad information, but stalled collective action around prevention and equity.",
      "x": 17957.04328072415,
      "y": -402.3767425980204,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_87646dc9-b17c-492c-b88c-7c227e77a824",
        "item_43e75b21-cff3-466c-84cb-2019c873c478"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_a94957af-67a2-4c73-a777-7bce1f43e323",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Civic Duty Without a Script",
      "description": "People who want to show up as citizens lack an agreed-upon daily, weekly, or monthly practice, so participation becomes guesswork instead of routine. The result is overwhelm for the public and a wider sense that political life is too opaque to enter.",
      "x": 19636.111030708336,
      "y": 1538.041714376734,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_d190d89d-dd7d-4477-a828-e072bdede790",
        "item_3641c00b-42dd-4db7-9b46-b0b1e2cb1571"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_c6e0a94c-dd7e-4646-ac0a-0473df10cbd2",
      "@type": "tg:Pattern",
      "type": "Pattern",
      "tier": 2,
      "title": "credibility collapse in moments that require preventive action",
      "description": "People are forced to decide without a trusted, legible way to tell which claims deserve action, so disinformation can delay, derail, or reverse choices before harm becomes visible. In this pattern, equity and voting read more like downstream consequences than the mechanism itself",
      "x": 16035.48298174916,
      "y": -806.624420800865,
      "w": 300,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "card_bca2ee95-81ac-4e5a-bf28-836c299aa2ef",
        "card_06d62388-65e3-4bdd-b41e-e0ab3af481ef"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_b8c6223f-d13d-4c18-9f63-3b8744f2b7dc",
      "@type": "tg:Pattern",
      "type": "Pattern",
      "tier": 2,
      "title": "Public disagreement is happening inside systems that reward performance over reciprocity",
      "description": "Civic actors are asked to appear collaborative or truth-seeking, but the setting does not reliably require mutual listening, shared definitions, or trust in the same reality — so disagreement becomes theater, and information becomes contestable rather than collectively usable.",
      "x": 16022.320117487676,
      "y": -371.0617217267645,
      "w": 300,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "card_44347b9e-520d-446d-9b32-420f55611be9",
        "card_bca2ee95-81ac-4e5a-bf28-836c299aa2ef"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_ea2ca113-3d8a-41db-a352-a2add3c5444c",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Hidden Voting Barriers",
      "description": "Voters encounter rules or requirements only when they show up to cast a ballot, so the barrier is often invisible until access is already at risk. The consequence is confusion, delayed participation, and possible disenfranchisement.  ",
      "x": 15307.54541709482,
      "y": 1626.269692541298,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_074cfedd-3385-48da-a6a0-7745ab57c160",
        "item_e8d308d8-05bc-46af-af7e-9e9c188d72cd"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_c5c70fb2-2368-46bc-9d74-fb3a963d7492",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": " Rigged Competition, Weak Choice",
      "description": "Congressional candidates face districts shaped by gerrymandering and money, so the practical consequence is that voters have less real competition and less say in who can win. The pain shows up as elections that feel predetermined rather than contested.\n\nDonor influence and district design work together to narrow competition and protect incumbents. The result is a system where politicians can more easily select their voters than persuade them.",
      "x": 14681.432041565593,
      "y": 2319.9373571416163,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_8bdc1d6c-3384-4489-9257-4e27fe18bee7",
      "@type": "tg:Evidence",
      "type": "Evidence",
      "tier": 1,
      "title": "Money Blocks Electoral Competition",
      "description": "Candidates and challengers run into a system where campaign finance advantages and district design make elections less competitive. The consequence is a field tilted toward incumbents and away from meaningful contest.",
      "x": 15117.106333279622,
      "y": 3274.591444932431,
      "w": 280,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "item_f250483f-b49a-45e8-8d6c-77b19f64155f",
        "item_098322bf-87f0-495f-9a83-eaa6413b6b49"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_92ae078a-8cb3-48f2-8c61-661e586ad92d",
      "@type": "tg:Pattern",
      "type": "Pattern",
      "tier": 2,
      "title": "closed competition structure",
      "description": "The rules of districting and the money needed to contest elections jointly make most races effectively non-contestable. The deeper condition is not just “low competitiveness,” but a system that lets incumbents and party-aligned power holders shape the field before voters ever meaningfully choose.",
      "x": 14048.081196818308,
      "y": 3111.546389321666,
      "w": 300,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "card_8e97daae-2ab3-4b97-b2b2-74056289092a",
        "card_8bdc1d6c-3384-4489-9257-4e27fe18bee7",
        "card_c5c70fb2-2368-46bc-9d74-fb3a963d7492"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_9a0d5f70-f818-44d8-b5b3-b37e279b8000",
      "@type": "tg:Pattern",
      "type": "Pattern",
      "tier": 2,
      "title": "Voters are being treated as subjects of administrative and structural control, not as equal authors of outcomes",
      "description": "Access and influence are both pre-shaped by authorities who set eligibility rules and electoral geometry before individual choice enters the picture.",
      "x": 14692.453561837783,
      "y": 1810.6030778049271,
      "w": 300,
      "h": 150,
      "cardType": null,
      "locked": false,
      "children": [
        "card_ea2ca113-3d8a-41db-a352-a2add3c5444c",
        "card_c5c70fb2-2368-46bc-9d74-fb3a963d7492",
        "card_8e97daae-2ab3-4b97-b2b2-74056289092a"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "card_16fc9b0f-da0f-44cd-b3e2-fdd290c4a191",
      "@type": "tg:Supertheme",
      "type": "Super-theme",
      "tier": 4,
